1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:05.472
Welcome to Lab 4, Document Understanding with Agentic Document Extraction.

2
00:00:06.172 --> 00:00:08.174
In this lab, you're going to be using

3
00:00:08.274 --> 00:00:11.378
the API-based version of Agentic Document Extraction

4
00:00:11.478 --> 00:00:14.180
from LandingAI. So, let's get started.

5
00:00:14.514 --> 00:00:16.416
All right, at the top of our

6
00:00:16.416 --> 00:00:18.318
notebook, a couple cells to do setup.

7
00:00:18.518 --> 00:00:21.021
Most of these should be familiar data science libraries

8
00:00:21.021 --> 00:00:23.323
or things that we used in previous lessons.

9
00:00:23.723 --> 00:00:27.327
New in this lesson are the imports here from LandingAI.

10
00:00:27.727 --> 00:00:31.331
So we're going to be importing landingai_ade

11
00:00:31.331 --> 00:00:33.333
and then importing specific

12
00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:36.236
types that we'll use for parsing and extracting.

13
00:00:36.636 --> 00:00:39.105
Next, you'll load the API key from the environment.

14
00:00:39.406 --> 00:00:41.908
Note that if you'd like to create your own API key

15
00:00:41.908 --> 00:00:43.910
to work with Agentic Document Extraction

16
00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:48.214
outside of this lesson, there will be instructions provided in the notebook.

17
00:00:48.515 --> 00:00:51.918
Let's initialize the client and now our setup is ready.

18
00:00:52.318 --> 00:00:55.121
So in a continuation of the setup,

19
00:00:55.121 --> 00:00:58.124
we're going to import a variety of helper functions.

20
00:00:58.525 --> 00:01:01.528
You have the ability to inspect the contents of all of these.

21
00:01:01.928 --> 00:01:03.930
It's going to keep the notebook a bit cleaner.

22
00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:06.132
Most of these are really for the

23
00:01:06.132 --> 00:01:08.435
purposes of drawing and displaying things on screen.

24
00:01:08.802 --> 00:01:11.304
In this lesson there are three exercises

25
00:01:11.604 --> 00:01:14.607
and each of them are broken into component parts.

26
00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:17.811
So the first exercise will be extracting verifiable

27
00:01:18.311 --> 00:01:21.014
key-value pairs from a utility bill,

28
00:01:21.314 --> 00:01:24.617
and of course the first step is to just preview the document.

29
00:01:25.018 --> 00:01:28.121
Here we use one of the helper functions to view the document.

30
00:01:28.555 --> 00:01:31.057
This is a combined gas and electric bill

31
00:01:31.057 --> 00:01:33.359
from a utility in San Diego.

32
00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:36.763
Few things to notice, it's for a particular monthly period.

33
00:01:37.263 --> 00:01:40.767
It does have both the gas and the electric charges separate,

34
00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:44.571
and it also has a usage history for both of those services.

35
00:01:45.105 --> 00:01:47.907
All right, in this section we'll be parsing the document.

36
00:01:48.608 --> 00:01:51.111
We'll be using the document pre-trained transformer

37
00:01:51.111 --> 00:01:53.613
that we introduced in the slide section.

38
00:01:54.314 --> 00:01:56.616
Since we've already imported ParseResponse,

39
00:01:57.117 --> 00:02:00.620
actually calling the API is just a matter of saying parse,

40
00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:04.624
specifying the document path and the model type.

41
00:02:04.724 --> 00:02:07.227
We'll be using dpt-2-latest.

42
00:02:07.827 --> 00:02:11.531
We release updates to dpt-2 roughly once per month.

43
00:02:11.731 --> 00:02:14.134
So your results may vary from mine.

44
00:02:14.734 --> 00:02:17.737
After we send the utility bill to the Parse API,

45
00:02:17.837 --> 00:02:20.540
we'll print a few details from the response.

46
00:02:21.141 --> 00:02:22.842
All right, our parsing is complete.

47
00:02:22.942 --> 00:02:25.945
We have a job ID we can refer back to.

48
00:02:26.246 --> 00:02:28.248
It was indeed just one page,

49
00:02:28.348 --> 00:02:32.018
resulted in about 6,000 characters and 24 chunks.

50
00:02:32.051 --> 00:02:35.555
So now we can continue to work with that parse result

51
00:02:35.855 --> 00:02:38.958
and inspect it. As you explore the output from parse,

52
00:02:39.058 --> 00:02:40.793
you'll see that it's very easy

53
00:02:40.793 --> 00:02:42.829
to overlay the results on the original,

54
00:02:43.029 --> 00:02:45.632
print some of the chunks and chunk summaries,

55
00:02:45.832 --> 00:02:47.534
and also print some of the markdown.

56
00:02:47.534 --> 00:02:49.235
Let's go ahead and do that now.

57
00:02:49.669 --> 00:02:53.039
All right, here is the annotated version of the document.

58
00:02:53.373 --> 00:02:55.441
And you'll notice bounding boxes of

59
00:02:55.441 --> 00:02:57.844
different colors for the different chunk types.

60
00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:00.446
So logo here in light green,

61
00:03:00.747 --> 00:03:02.649
text in the medium green,

62
00:03:03.049 --> 00:03:05.351
and notice also that each chunk has

63
00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:08.154
a unique identifier in addition to the type.

64
00:03:08.354 --> 00:03:11.858
Here we see a table chunk and notably inside the table chunk,

65
00:03:11.858 --> 00:03:16.162
there are individual table cells that have their own unique identifiers.

66
00:03:16.863 --> 00:03:20.867
In purple, a margin chunk and of course two figure chunks.

67
00:03:21.968 --> 00:03:25.471
In the JSON response, chunks is one of the top-level elements.

68
00:03:25.672 --> 00:03:27.273
And here we're printing the first

69
00:03:27.273 --> 00:03:29.175
five, so we can see the structure.

70
00:03:30.176 --> 00:03:32.979
Recall from the original that in the top left corner

71
00:03:33.179 --> 00:03:36.349
there is a logo, and that happens to be chunk zero.

72
00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:39.552
So here we're looking at the identifier, the type,

73
00:03:39.752 --> 00:03:43.556
the page and the coordinates corresponding all to chunk zero.

74
00:03:43.856 --> 00:03:46.759
The JSON output is well structured,

75
00:03:46.759 --> 00:03:48.861
allowing you to do simple aggregations such as this one

76
00:03:48.861 --> 00:03:51.664
where you may count the chunks of each type.

77
00:03:52.065 --> 00:03:54.968
Another top-level element is the markdown.

78
00:03:55.268 --> 00:03:59.072
And here we're displaying the first 500 characters of the markdown.

79
00:03:59.472 --> 00:04:03.176
Conveniently, the identifier is included along with the markdown content

80
00:04:03.176 --> 00:04:05.378
for each one of these chunks.

81
00:04:05.778 --> 00:04:09.482
In this cell, we're not looking at the top-level markdown element,

82
00:04:09.482 --> 00:04:13.553
we're actually looking at chunks again and chunk number nine.

83
00:04:14.087 --> 00:04:16.189
So what's important to learn here

84
00:04:16.189 --> 00:04:18.191
is that within the chunks element,

85
00:04:18.191 --> 00:04:21.894
each of the individual markdowns are available separately.

86
00:04:22.295 --> 00:04:24.097
This one happens to be a table,

87
00:04:24.097 --> 00:04:26.499
which renders nicely as HTML.

88
00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:29.202
Recall when we looked at the overlays

89
00:04:29.202 --> 00:04:31.604
that I drew your attention to the fact

90
00:04:31.604 --> 00:04:34.307
that each table cell has its own identifier.

91
00:04:34.807 --> 00:04:37.310
Here's the raw markdown for that same chunk.

92
00:04:37.777 --> 00:04:40.980
And here you can really see each of those table identifiers.

93
00:04:41.581 --> 00:04:44.284
This is going to be helpful later for visual grounding

94
00:04:44.584 --> 00:04:47.587
because we can refer back to a specific table cell

95
00:04:47.587 --> 00:04:50.790
by its identifier once we extract a value.

96
00:04:51.291 --> 00:04:55.395
Having completed parsing, it's time to extract those key-value pairs.

97
00:04:55.762 --> 00:04:57.864
If you remember from the slides,

98
00:04:57.864 --> 00:05:00.667
the extraction step combines the markdown

99
00:05:00.667 --> 00:05:03.236
from the parse step together with a schema.

100
00:05:03.536 --> 00:05:05.738
So we'll need to define a schema

101
00:05:05.738 --> 00:05:07.740
that corresponds to the utility bill.

102
00:05:08.241 --> 00:05:10.243
This schema has 10 properties

103
00:05:10.543 --> 00:05:12.445
and is defined using JSON.

104
00:05:12.845 --> 00:05:16.416
The important things to note are that you can have nested schemas.

105
00:05:16.916 --> 00:05:21.321
Here under account_summary, we have nested underneath the current_charges

106
00:05:21.321 --> 00:05:24.023
and the total_amount_due.

107
00:05:24.324 --> 00:05:27.827
Under gas_summary, notice that we can also have booleans

108
00:05:27.827 --> 00:05:30.296
and strings and numerous

109
00:05:30.296 --> 00:05:33.232
other types. And notice that each field has

110
00:05:33.499 --> 00:05:36.969
a fairly robust description. The more descriptive you are,

111
00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:39.305
the more likely you are to get what you want

112
00:05:39.539 --> 00:05:41.274
when you go to extract these fields.

113
00:05:41.874 --> 00:05:44.010
The last line here converts the dictionary

114
00:05:44.177 --> 00:05:47.380
into a JSON-formatted string to use with the API.

115
00:05:47.747 --> 00:05:50.550
Now you're ready to call the extract API

116
00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:52.352
using ExtractResponse.

117
00:05:52.852 --> 00:05:54.954
The JSON schema has been defined

118
00:05:55.555 --> 00:05:58.558
and the parse_result is still available in memory.

119
00:05:58.958 --> 00:06:02.462
Again, we'll be using the latest version of the extraction model,

120
00:06:02.462 --> 00:06:04.764
so your results might differ slightly.

121
00:06:05.365 --> 00:06:08.468
With the extraction complete, it's time to explore the result.

122
00:06:08.868 --> 00:06:10.970
I'm expecting 10 total values

123
00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:14.474
with a mixture of numerics, booleans, and strings.

124
00:06:15.174 --> 00:06:17.443
And this appears to be exactly what I wanted.

125
00:06:17.744 --> 00:06:20.146
At the top level account_summary,

126
00:06:20.146 --> 00:06:23.449
current_charges are $155.15.

127
00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:26.052
which also equals the amount due.

128
00:06:26.452 --> 00:06:30.456
The booleans are telling us that a usage chart is available

129
00:06:30.556 --> 00:06:32.959
and the max month is actually

130
00:06:32.959 --> 00:06:35.762
interpreting a value from the usage chart,

131
00:06:35.762 --> 00:06:37.797
saying that in the last 12

132
00:06:37.797 --> 00:06:40.166
months, January was the highest gas consumption

133
00:06:40.266 --> 00:06:43.069
and August was the highest electric consumption.

134
00:06:43.569 --> 00:06:46.072
You can go back to the original and verify that

135
00:06:46.072 --> 00:06:48.374
based on the peaks in the bar chart.

136
00:06:49.075 --> 00:06:52.478
Then, in the extraction metadata, we see the references

137
00:06:52.478 --> 00:06:54.781
for where that information was obtained.

138
00:06:55.114 --> 00:06:57.917
This 0-a is one of the table cells

139
00:06:57.917 --> 00:06:59.619
from chunk 9

140
00:06:59.619 --> 00:07:02.321
and as is 0-d.

141
00:07:02.321 --> 00:07:05.124
So these values are coming from a table.

142
00:07:05.491 --> 00:07:08.795
Typically when you see a longer chunk reference like this,

143
00:07:08.995 --> 00:07:11.097
it's coming from a figure, a text

144
00:07:11.097 --> 00:07:13.199
block, or something that's not a table.

145
00:07:13.499 --> 00:07:16.202
These two responses combined with the parse result

146
00:07:16.302 --> 00:07:18.504
give me all the information I would need

147
00:07:18.704 --> 00:07:20.706
to actually build a user interface

148
00:07:21.007 --> 00:07:24.310
to display back to the user the fields that they wanted

149
00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:27.513
and exactly the portion of the document that that came from.

150
00:07:28.047 --> 00:07:30.116
So to wrap up exercise one,

151
00:07:30.183 --> 00:07:33.820
you've used two APIs, parse and extract.

152
00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:36.923
It's really parse that is analogous

153
00:07:36.989 --> 00:07:39.826
to what we did in lessons one, two, and three.

154
00:07:40.393 --> 00:07:44.997
But here the Agentic Document Extraction API has abstracted away

155
00:07:45.097 --> 00:07:47.700
all of the model training, configuration,

156
00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:50.670
and custom coding that was previously required.

157
00:07:51.070 --> 00:07:53.806
and a single API call is all

158
00:07:53.806 --> 00:07:56.542
that's required to understand the entire document.

159
00:07:57.043 --> 00:07:59.045
And the output is better.

160
00:07:59.545 --> 00:08:02.248
The quality difference will become more apparent

161
00:08:02.248 --> 00:08:05.551
as you test more difficult documents in exercise two.

162
00:08:06.552 --> 00:08:08.955
We're in section four, exercise two,

163
00:08:08.955 --> 00:08:12.558
and we'll look at the agentic document extraction performance

164
00:08:12.558 --> 00:08:15.661
on some more difficult documents. We've selected

165
00:08:15.695 --> 00:08:18.898
six for you that are difficult in various ways.

166
00:08:19.198 --> 00:08:22.101
But the same API using the same underlying

167
00:08:22.201 --> 00:08:25.805
Document Pretrained Transformer can understand all of them.

168
00:08:26.305 --> 00:08:28.508
I'll be moving through this section fairly quickly,

169
00:08:28.608 --> 00:08:31.611
but feel free to take your time and explore these examples.

170
00:08:32.178 --> 00:08:34.180
There's one long function here

171
00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:37.950
which is going to call the parse API for each document

172
00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:40.152
and print a variety of outputs.

173
00:08:40.453 --> 00:08:43.956
That's going to allow us to quickly run all six of them

174
00:08:43.956 --> 00:08:45.558
using this function repeatedly.

175
00:08:45.658 --> 00:08:49.262
This section is really about looking at a wide variety of examples.

176
00:08:49.462 --> 00:08:51.764
Feel free to substitute some of your own.

177
00:08:51.964 --> 00:08:54.166
For each one, after I execute the cell,

178
00:08:54.467 --> 00:08:57.670
I'll just draw your attention to one or two key differentiators.

179
00:08:58.271 --> 00:09:00.640
Let's start with some charts and flowcharts.

180
00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:03.042
This is what the original looks like.

181
00:09:03.276 --> 00:09:06.245
It's an interior page of an investor presentation.

182
00:09:07.046 --> 00:09:09.649
And ADE has found nine total chunks

183
00:09:09.849 --> 00:09:12.351
and has correctly detected all of the figures.

184
00:09:13.419 --> 00:09:15.888
Let's select this figure down in the lower right

185
00:09:15.888 --> 00:09:18.891
and see if it's understanding this horizontal line

186
00:09:18.891 --> 00:09:21.794
as the average at 4.3x

187
00:09:22.094 --> 00:09:24.897
And indeed, there it is. Within the bar chart type

188
00:09:24.897 --> 00:09:27.700
Average Revenue Multiple of 4.3x

189
00:09:28.234 --> 00:09:30.636
Let's take a look at this flowchart original.

190
00:09:31.537 --> 00:09:35.141
Here's an example of a document that a human understands intuitively,

191
00:09:35.141 --> 00:09:39.145
but has a lot of spatial and relationship-based information

192
00:09:39.145 --> 00:09:42.548
with the arrows and pointing at all sorts of different directions.

193
00:09:43.049 --> 00:09:46.118
ADE detects all of this as one large figure,

194
00:09:46.519 --> 00:09:49.422
which is correct. It is one large visual unit.

195
00:09:49.956 --> 00:09:53.459
And let's see if we can find where Good reference

196
00:09:53.859 --> 00:09:56.562
follows the yes to Select candidate.

197
00:09:57.163 --> 00:10:00.166
This would be very difficult in traditional OCR

198
00:10:00.166 --> 00:10:04.470
because Select candidate is actually higher on the page than Good reference.

199
00:10:05.037 --> 00:10:07.640
And here it is. Good reference?

200
00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:10.643
If yes, it proceeds to select candidate.

201
00:10:11.043 --> 00:10:13.346
Well done. Let's look at some tables.

202
00:10:13.646 --> 00:10:16.048
The world is full of tables, but in

203
00:10:16.048 --> 00:10:18.451
many cases, some of the lines are missing.

204
00:10:18.451 --> 00:10:22.655
This table has no column dividers and no row dividers,

205
00:10:22.955 --> 00:10:25.458
and because of the large amount of blank space,

206
00:10:25.458 --> 00:10:27.927
it's very difficult as an OCR problem.

207
00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:30.196
In the response, you can see that

208
00:10:30.196 --> 00:10:32.431
the entire section was detected as a table.

209
00:10:32.932 --> 00:10:34.467
And you can see that all

210
00:10:34.467 --> 00:10:36.235
of the blank cells have been understood

211
00:10:36.235 --> 00:10:39.238
because of the regular array of cells that we see here.

212
00:10:39.639 --> 00:10:42.942
Printing the markdown as HTML to the notebook

213
00:10:43.242 --> 00:10:44.944
shows all of those blanks.

214
00:10:44.944 --> 00:10:47.046
What about something like this?

215
00:10:47.046 --> 00:10:48.948
You could even call this a mega table.

216
00:10:49.248 --> 00:10:51.951
There are over a thousand individual cells here.

217
00:10:52.351 --> 00:10:54.954
In addition to the total quantity of cells,

218
00:10:55.154 --> 00:10:57.857
notice also that the rows have merged cells

219
00:10:58.057 --> 00:11:00.059
and the columns have merged cells.

220
00:11:00.426 --> 00:11:02.328
All right, we're certainly not going to

221
00:11:02.328 --> 00:11:04.230
go through and verify every cell here,

222
00:11:04.630 --> 00:11:08.634
but if you've ever tried to extract a mega table like this

223
00:11:08.934 --> 00:11:13.239
with an LLM, you'll know that it's very susceptible to hallucinations.

224
00:11:13.506 --> 00:11:18.310
The LLM simply cannot hold this many numbers in its context window.

225
00:11:18.711 --> 00:11:21.013
And this is really where the agentic approach

226
00:11:21.013 --> 00:11:23.015
becomes crucially important.

227
00:11:23.716 --> 00:11:26.318
And of course, if you needed your mega table

228
00:11:26.318 --> 00:11:29.021
actually rendered in Excel or HTML,

229
00:11:29.321 --> 00:11:31.724
we could now quickly save this as CSV.

230
00:11:32.158 --> 00:11:36.462
Continuing with the theme of handwriting, but taking it to another level.

231
00:11:37.063 --> 00:11:38.964
Here's a document with handwriting.

232
00:11:39.365 --> 00:11:40.966
It also has a checkbox.

233
00:11:40.966 --> 00:11:43.269
It also has six or seven

234
00:11:43.269 --> 00:11:45.971
instances of the word no being circled,

235
00:11:46.372 --> 00:11:48.974
and it has four pre-existing conditions.

236
00:11:49.275 --> 00:11:53.245
Asthma, circulation, eye infection, and urinary tract infection.

237
00:11:53.646 --> 00:11:55.948
Let's take a look at how this gets parsed.

238
00:11:56.482 --> 00:11:59.285
For this example, it's not the chunk identification

239
00:11:59.485 --> 00:12:01.487
that is the impressive part.

240
00:12:01.987 --> 00:12:04.156
It's the markdown itself and how

241
00:12:04.156 --> 00:12:06.692
it detects the checkboxes and the circles.

242
00:12:07.293 --> 00:12:09.295
So let's scroll down to that area.

243
00:12:09.662 --> 00:12:12.965
Here's the one yes no checkbox. Do you have a Pacemaker?

244
00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:18.471
Here we see the seven instances of No having been circled.

245
00:12:19.472 --> 00:12:22.475
And here we see the section where the patient is instructed

246
00:12:22.475 --> 00:12:24.677
to circle the pre-existing conditions.

247
00:12:25.177 --> 00:12:28.481
And we do see Asthma, circulation, Eye Infection,

248
00:12:28.481 --> 00:12:30.683
and Urinary Infection as marked.

249
00:12:31.217 --> 00:12:35.087
This is the type of output that combined with an LLM

250
00:12:35.087 --> 00:12:37.623
can now accurately extract medical information

251
00:12:37.623 --> 00:12:39.525
in a very reliable way.

252
00:12:40.092 --> 00:12:42.194
Let's push the boundaries on handwriting

253
00:12:42.194 --> 00:12:43.996
with some integral calculus.

254
00:12:44.697 --> 00:12:47.800
Let me draw your attention to this portion of the handwritten response.

255
00:12:48.701 --> 00:12:51.704
This is the square root of root 2 over 2.

256
00:12:52.404 --> 00:12:54.707
has a lot of visual information encoded here.

257
00:12:55.007 --> 00:12:57.009
So let's look for this in the response.

258
00:12:57.543 --> 00:12:59.745
All right, the bounding boxes look good.

